  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 8,
    "lesson_title": "Phase Shifting by $\\angle H(j\\omega)$",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the output sinusoid's phase by evaluating $\\angle H(j\\omega)$ at the input frequency and adding that shift to the input phase while leaving the frequency unchanged.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Phase Shifting by $\\angle H(j\\omega)$",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Phase Shifting by $\\angle H(j\\omega)$",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The previous lesson showed that a complex exponential input keeps the same frequency through an LTI system. Here the new focus is the phase term: the system response $H(j\\omega)$ rotates the output at that input frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write $H(j\\omega)$ in polar form so the phase contribution is visible. That lets you see the response as a magnitude factor together with an angle that will be added to the output exponent.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j\\omega)=|H(j\\omega)|e^{j\\angle H(j\\omega)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now multiply the input $e^{j\\omega t}$ by that polar form. The angle from $H(j\\omega)$ adds directly to the exponent, so the output keeps the same $\\omega$ and picks up a phase rotation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=H(j\\omega)e^{j\\omega t}=|H(j\\omega)|e^{j(\\omega t+\\angle H(j\\omega))}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a real sinusoid, read the same rule as a phase shift on the cosine. Add $\\angle H(j\\omega)$ to the input phase $\\phi$ and keep $\\omega$ unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad y(t)=A|H(j\\omega)|\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi+\\angle H(j\\omega))",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the reusable rule is: evaluate $\\angle H(j\\omega)$ at the input frequency, add it to the input phase, and keep the frequency the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Phase Shifting by $\\angle H(j\\omega)$",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Adding a Given Phase Shift to an Input Phase",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)=\\cos(4t+\\pi/6)$ and $\\angle H(j4)=\\pi/3$, what is the output phase?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the system phase shift is already given at the input frequency, so the only move is to add the angles and keep $\\omega=4$ unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\phi_{\\text{out}}=\\pi/6+\\pi/3=\\pi/2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output phase is $\\pi/2$. The output sinusoid still uses the same frequency $4$, so the full cosine form changes only in its phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\cos(4t+\\pi/2)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Phase Shifting by $\\angle H(j\\omega)$",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Adding a Negative Phase Shift",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)=\\cos(2t+\\pi/4)$ and $\\angle H(j2)=-\\pi/6$, what is the output phase?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the system phase shift is already given at the input frequency, so a negative value is still added to the input phase. Keep $\\omega=2$ unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\phi_{\\text{out}}=\\pi/4+(-\\pi/6)=\\pi/12",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output phase is $\\pi/12$. The negative sign changes the angle, but it does not change the frequency of the sinusoid.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\cos(2t+\\pi/12)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Phase Shifting by $\\angle H(j\\omega)$",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Evaluating $\\angle H(j\\omega)$ at the Input Frequency",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)=\\cos(t+\\pi/6)$ and $H(j\\omega)=\\frac{1+j\\omega}{1-j\\omega}$, what is the output phase?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that $\\angle H(j\\omega)$ is not given directly, so first evaluate the transfer function at the input frequency $\\omega=1$ and read off its angle. Then add that angle to the input phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\angle H(j1)=\\angle\\!\\left(\\frac{1+j}{1-j}\\right)=\\angle(1+j)-\\angle(1-j)=\\frac{\\pi}{4}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\pi}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now add the evaluated phase shift to the input phase $\\pi/6$. The frequency stays at $1$ while only the angle changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\phi_{\\text{out}}=\\pi/6+\\pi/2=2\\pi/3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output phase is $2\\pi/3$, and the same-frequency output would be $\\cos(t+2\\pi/3)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Phase Shifting by $\\angle H(j\\omega)$",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Writing the Same-Frequency Output with the Shifted Phase",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)=A\\cos(5t-\\pi/3)$ and $\\angle H(j5)=\\pi/6$, write the output sinusoid.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the phase result from the previous step, then put it back into the cosine. The frequency stays at $5$, so only the phase term changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\phi_{\\text{out}}=-\\pi/3+\\pi/6=-\\pi/6",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output is still the same kind of sinusoid: same amplitude symbol $A$, same angular frequency $5$, and only the phase updated to $-\\pi/6$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=A\\cos(5t-\\pi/6)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
